  "referred_to_by": [
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "The remainder that resists full transfer between languages. In Dictee, untranslatability works as a textual strategy rather than a failure: French and English are set on facing pages without equivalence, so that homophones such as cygnes/signes and tense distinctions such as the imperfect j'écoutais against the simple past \"I heard\" leave a political residue that neither language can absorb. Distinct from dict:concept/translational-violence, which names the loss imposed by colonial language policy; untranslatability names what the text withholds in response. Draws on Emily Apter, Against World Literature: On the Politics of Untranslatability (2013), and Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak, \"The Politics of Translation\" (1993).",
      "classified_as": "description"
    }
  ],
  "dictrel:evidenceType": "Modeling"
}